<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sovereign Principality of the United Netherlands</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a>–<a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sovereign Principality of the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was the state formed in the northern Netherlands after French rule ended in 1813. William, son of the last stadtholder, returned as sovereign prince and accepted a constitution in 1814. In March 1815 he proclaimed himself King William I of the United Kingdom of the Netherlands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After Prussian and Russian troops drove the French from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in 1813, a provisional government was headed by a triumvirate of noblemen: Frans Adam van der Duyn van Maasdam, Leopold of Limburg Stirum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gijsbert-karel-van-hogendorp">Gijsbert Karel van Hogendorp</a>. It took formal control on 20 November and declared the principality the next day. Many of its members had helped drive out the stadtholder William V eighteen years earlier, but most agreed it was better to invite his son William Frederick themselves than to have him imposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p><p>William Frederick returned from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and landed from HMS Warrior on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheveningen">Scheveningen</a> beach on 30 November 1813. He declined the triumvirate's offer to make him king and on 2 December proclaimed himself Sovereign Prince of the United Netherlands, insisting on a wise constitution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A commission under van Hogendorp drafted the constitution, and on 29 March 1814 an Assembly of Notables met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> to vote on it: 474 of its 600 members attended, and only 26, mostly Roman Catholics, voted against. William Frederick was inaugurated by the assembly in the New Church in Amsterdam on 30 March.</p><p>The constitution set up a centralised monarchy in which the prince held much power. A single-chamber States General of 55 members, elected by the States-Provincial, had the right of initiative and had to approve all legislation, but the prince could settle many matters by decree. Provinces and cities had far less power than under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, while freedom of religion and other rights of the French period were kept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The former Austrian Netherlands, conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1794 and annexed in 1795, were also taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, who in February 1814 set up a provisional military government there. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> showed little interest in returning, the Allies gave the country to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> state in secret annexes to the Treaty of Chaumont, and the Treaty of Paris of 30 May 1814 assigned it the land on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>. Under the Eight Articles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> of 21 June 1814 William Frederick received the former Austrian Netherlands, becoming Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> on 1 August.</p><p>His attempt to win the assent of carefully selected Belgian representatives to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> constitution met opposition from those who feared for the position of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. The union was nonetheless completed on 16 March 1815, when he was proclaimed king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. The principality is the direct legal ancestor of the modern Dutch state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sovereign Principality of the United Netherlands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
